// #3564. Behavioural counterpart to tests/bit/pollguardoffsets.bit's ninth
// (wkId) offset, in the same shape tests/bit/pollguardstopword.bit already
// uses for the worldStopWord clause: pollguardoffsets.bit only compares
// LITERALS (this file's copy against compiler/codegen.bit's, parsed as
// text), and never runs a single compiled program, so it cannot tell
// whether the COMPILED back-edge poll guard actually observes and acts on
// a pending preempt request. This gate closes that for clause 3
// (compiler/arm64call.bit's emitInlinedPreemptCheck, mirroring
// runtime/stw/stwpoll.bit's #3560 clause exactly).
//
// #3560's own runtime-level fix, alone, could not be tested end to end:
// nothing compiled calls stwPollNeeded (compiler/codegen.bit:322 — dead
// since #2925/#3169), so #3560's ticket recorded a deterministic harness
// driving stwPollNeeded directly instead. THIS gate drives the same
// scenario through the REAL compiled path: a spinning, allocation-free
// mutator with a genuine loop back edge, so the compiler emits the inlined
// guard at it, and a real bl bit_rt_safepoint only fires if that guard's
// preempt clause actually returns true.
//
// WHY preemptStamp/sysmonTick DIRECTLY, not a real 10ms wait. Backdating the
// stamp (`monotonic().ns - preemptBudgetNs - 1`) then calling `sysmonTick`
// once sets the flag deterministically, the same way sysmonTick's own OS
// thread would after a real preemptBudgetNs of continuous running — see
// runtime/sched/preempt.bit's own comments on both functions. This is
// exactly what the ticket's acceptance criterion asks for: "a second task
// setting the preempt flag via preemptStamp/sysmonTick".
//
// WHY THE MUTATOR PUBLISHES ITS OWN WORKER ID, rather than this file
// scanning every slot. `schedCurrentWorker()` returns a raw, word-aligned
// WORKER BLOCK ADDRESS (never a small index — see runtime/sched/task.bit's
// own note on schedMaybeYieldForPreempt), so the small id `preemptStamp`/
// `preemptRequested`/`sysmonTick` index by is `*(taskAt(w) + wkId)` — the
// exact conversion the guard's own new clause performs. The mutator is the
// only task that can legitimately ask "what worker am I running on right
// now", and it publishes once, before it starts spinning; since nothing
// makes it yield until the fix under test fires, that worker binding cannot
// change out from under this file's later preemptStamp/sysmonTick call.
//
// THE CLAUSE THIS KILLS: clause 3, `preemptRequested(workerId)` (after
// schedCurrentWorker() != 0). MUTATION-PROVEN — see smash #3564: reverting
// compiler/arm64call.bit's emitInlinedPreemptCheck call site (the
// `pollFixups = emitInlinedPreemptCheck(cx, pollFixups)` line in
// emitInlinedPollGuard) and rebuilding makes this gate FAIL — the flag never
// clears within the deadline below, exit 1. Restoring it makes this gate
// PASS again, exit 0.
//
// REQUIRES BIT_WORKERS >= 2, same reason and same failure mode as
// pollguardstopword.bit's own header: with the scheduler's single-worker
// default the controller and the mutator can never run concurrently, so
// this hangs (bounded by the mutator's own iteration ceiling, not by this
// file) rather than failing fast. Set in this gate's own tools/build/gates.bit
// entry, not left to the caller's environment.

import { exit } from "std/os"
import { newWaitGroup, WaitGroup } from "std/sync"
import { monotonic, sleep, Millisecond, Second } from "std/time"
import {
  schedCurrentWorker, taskAt, wkId, schedMaxWorkers,
  preemptStamp, sysmonTick, preemptRequested, preemptBudgetNs,
} from "../../runtime/sched"

class Shared { wg: WaitGroup, started: []i64, testDone: []i64, workerId: []i64 }

// The mutator under test: allocation-free, with a real loop back edge so the
// compiler emits the inlined poll guard at it — see pollguardstopword.bit's
// mutatorLoop for why this shape (spawn, bounded spin, shared testDone) beats
// a userspace lock for isolating one clause.
fn mutatorLoop(s: Shared) {
  let raw = schedCurrentWorker()
  // raw==0 means off-task (see runtime/sched/task.bit's own note on
  // schedCurrentWorker — a small-index worker id of 0 is a perfectly valid
  // first worker and must NOT be confused with this). A properly dispatched
  // task can never observe raw==0; -1 is a sentinel the controller checks.
  let wid = -1
  if (raw != 0) {
    wid = *(taskAt(raw) + wkId)
  }
  atomicStore(ptrOf(s.workerId), wid)
  atomicStore(ptrOf(s.started), 1)
  let i = 0
  // Bounded per Power of 10 (rule 2); see pollguardstopword.bit's own note on
  // this exact ceiling and iteration rate. Never reached in a correctly
  // configured (BIT_WORKERS >= 2) run, win or lose.
  while (i < 2000000000 && atomicLoad(ptrOf(s.testDone)) == 0) {
    i = i + 1
  }
  s.wg.done()
}

fn waitForStart(started: []i64) {
  let startDeadline = monotonic().ns + 5 * Second
  while (atomicLoad(ptrOf(started)) == 0) {
    if (monotonic().ns > startDeadline) {
      println("pollguardpreempt: FAIL — mutator loop never started")
      exit(1)
    }
    sleep(1 * Millisecond)
  }
}

// Race-free setup proof by ELIMINATION (smash #3609): reset every OTHER
// worker slot's clock immediately before stamping wid, so the caller's
// flagged-count check is not satisfied by ambient noise, then deliberately
// backdate wid's own clock and return sysmonTick's own return value -- the
// only race-free signal available (see the caller's own comment on why a
// direct preemptRequested(wid) read here is racy against the guard under
// test). Bounded by schedMaxWorkers (32, a compile-time constant, Power of
// 10). Stamping an idle slot (no task ever dispatched there — this program
// spawns exactly one task besides main) is harmless: preempt.bit's only
// other reader of startNs is the scheduler's own dispatch-time preemptStamp
// call, which simply overwrites it again if that slot is ever really used.
//
// MUTATION-TESTED, two failure modes closed, one NOT (documented here
// rather than silently narrowed, per this ticket):
//   - CLOSED: preemptStamp given an insufficient backdate for wid itself
//     (simulating broken preemptStamp/sysmonTick arithmetic) — 5/5 caught
//     with this elimination loop, vs 2/3 with an earlier controller-only-
//     reset revision, because the CALLING task's own waitForStart+settle-
//     sleep stretch can independently put ITS OWN worker over
//     preemptBudgetNs and mask the bug via ambient noise.
//   - NOT CLOSED: preemptStamp given a WRONG (but valid, in-range) worker
//     index instead of wid — false-PASSED 3/3 even with this elimination
//     loop, because the wrongly-targeted slot's own artificial stamp
//     satisfies `flagged >= 1` exactly like a correct one would. No
//     aggregate count can distinguish "wid was flagged" from "some other
//     single worker was flagged instead" — that needs either a race-free
//     PER-WORKER read (no such accessor is exported from preempt.bit
//     today) or reading preemptRequested(wid) directly, which is exactly
//     the racy read this fix removes. This narrow class (a bug in the
//     literal worker-index argument at this function's own preemptStamp(wid,
//     ...) call, as opposed to a bug in preemptStamp/sysmonTick's own
//     arithmetic) is left uncaught by this self-check.
fn armPreemptFor(wid: i64): i64 {
  let w = 0
  while (w < schedMaxWorkers) {
    if (w != wid) {
      preemptStamp(w, monotonic().ns)
    }
    w = w + 1
  }
  // A second task (this one) sets the preempt flag directly, the same way
  // sysmonTick's own OS thread would after preemptBudgetNs of real time.
  preemptStamp(wid, monotonic().ns - preemptBudgetNs - 1)
  return sysmonTick(monotonic().ns)
}

fn main() {
  let started = []i64(1)
  let testDone = []i64(1)
  let workerId = []i64(1)
  let wg = newWaitGroup()
  wg.add(1)
  spawn mutatorLoop(Shared{ wg: wg, started: started, testDone: testDone, workerId: workerId })

  waitForStart(started)
  sleep(20 * Millisecond) // settle solidly inside the loop, not its first iteration

  let wid = atomicLoad(ptrOf(workerId))
  if (wid < 0) {
    println("pollguardpreempt: FAIL — mutator's schedCurrentWorker() returned 0 (off-task); a spawned, dispatched task should never observe this")
    exit(1)
  }

  // `flagged` (sysmonTick's own return value, via armPreemptFor -- see its
  // doc comment for the full race-freeness and mutation-testing rationale)
  // is the setup-correctness signal here, deliberately NOT a direct read of
  // preemptRequested(wid) (smash #3609: that used to be this check, and it
  // false-failed once under heavy fleet contention, "setup broken ...
  // preemptRequested(0) is false right after sysmonTick flagged 2
  // worker(s)" -- a fast, CORRECT guard clearing the flag before this
  // thread's next instruction runs). `flagged == 0` here can only mean
  // sysmonTick's own scan is broken, never that the guard raced ahead.
  let flagged = armPreemptFor(wid)
  if (flagged == 0) {
    atomicStore(ptrOf(testDone), 1)
    wg.wait()
    println("pollguardpreempt: FAIL — setup broken, not the guard under test: sysmonTick flagged 0 worker(s) despite worker ${wid} being deliberately backdated past preemptBudgetNs via preemptStamp")
    exit(1)
  }

  // Poll (bounded by wall-clock deadline, the same shape waitForStart above
  // uses) for the COMPILED guard to observe and clear it. Only
  // compiler/arm64call.bit's emitInlinedPreemptCheck reaching bit_rt_safepoint
  // -> schedMaybeYieldForPreempt -> maybePreempt can make this clear — that
  // is the actual thing under test. It is legitimate for `cleared` to go
  // true on the very FIRST check below: the `flagged >= 1` proof above
  // already establishes the flag really was set for wid specifically, and
  // the only thing that clears requested[wid] while the mutator's task is
  // still alive is that same guard's maybePreempt call — so an instant
  // "already false" here means the guard won the race, not that setup was
  // skipped.
  let deadline = monotonic().ns + 2 * Second
  let cleared = false
  while (!cleared && monotonic().ns < deadline) {
    if (!preemptRequested(wid)) {
      cleared = true
    } else {
      sleep(1 * Millisecond)
    }
  }

  atomicStore(ptrOf(testDone), 1)
  wg.wait()

  if (!cleared) {
    println("pollguardpreempt: FAIL — preemptRequested(${wid}) never cleared; the compiled back-edge poll guard did not observe the pending preempt (compiler/arm64call.bit's emitInlinedPreemptCheck is missing, wrong, or compiler/codegen.bit's pgWkIdWord has drifted)")
    exit(1)
  }
  println("pollguardpreempt: PASS — allocation-free mutator's own back-edge poll guard observed and cleared the pending preempt for worker ${wid}")
}
